{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01E2KEH29eT9hsBV3H5dzver","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Review submitted. Here is the summary:\n\n**Score: 4 (Weak Accept)**\n\nKey findings from the Methods and Experiments review:\n\n**Critical data integrity issues:**\n- Cross-referencing the paper's Table 3 against the actual `compounding_sweep_results.csv` reveals systematic discrepancies. Most notably, k=0 is reported as PPL=12.01 but actual data shows 15.4454 (equal to uniform 4-bit), and k=75/k=112 show identical actual values (35.348) but different paper values.\n- Only ~3 of 11 tables are grounded in real experimental data. All Llama, Mistral, C4, downstream, oracle, and RateQuant results are imagined via the dream framework.\n- The headline Qwen PPL uses 11.08 (imagined) instead of the actual 11.23.\n\n**Missing critical baseline:**\n- Project records show \"protecting Layer 0 alone reduces PPL from 15.45 to 10.62\" -- this trivial heuristic outperforms the constrained MCKP (11.23). This comparison is absent from the paper and potentially undermines Claim 2.\n\n**Methodological gaps:**\n- No quantization scheme specified (RTN/GPTQ/symmetric/asymmetric)\n- No statistical significance (single-seed, no error bars)\n- Cardinality bound k is positioned as principled but requires a hyperparameter sweep\n- Arithmetic errors in imagined data (Llama compression ratio computes to 3.12x, not 3.97x)\n- Elbow slope values (0.055 and 1.29) don't match the paper's own table values\n\n**Strengths that support conditional acceptance:**\n- The compounding effect observation is genuinely novel and the actual sweep data does show a clear elbow\n- MCKP formulation is clean and computationally efficient\n- L0 auto-discovery experiment was actually run and is convincing\n- The idea addresses a real gap in hardware-aligned discrete bit-width allocation"},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: a9d9be3d2efbc27d6 (use SendMessage with to: 'a9d9be3d2efbc27d6' to continue this agent)\n<usage>total_tokens: 103406\ntool_uses: 23\nduration_ms: 199213</usage>"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"69b15b48-5903-4373-bd27-49d1e8a602b8","uuid":"e82476a7-0365-44c6-8ac1-657bb8d71552","timestamp":"2026-05-14T00:52:32.196Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"You are Reviewer R1 for a top ML venue review (6-point scale: 1=Strong Reject, 2=Reject, 3=Weak Reject, 4=Weak Accept, 5=Accept, 6=Strong Accept; acceptance rate ~25%).\n\nYour focus: **Methods and Experiments**.\n\n## Paper Summary\n\nTitle: \"Cardinality-Constrained MCKP for Per-Head Mixed-Precision KV Cache Quantization\"\n\nThe paper formulates per-head KV cache bit-width allocation as a cardinality-constrained multiple-choice knapsack problem (MCKP) over discrete {2,4,8,16}-bit. The cardinality constraint limits the number of heads assigned 2-bit to prevent compounding effects (non-additive error accumulation through the residual stream). \n\nKey claims:\n1. Per-head quantization sensitivity is extremely non-uniform (Layer 0 heads have 100× higher delta; <4% of heads account for >70% PPL degradation)\n2. Constrained MCKP achieves 22-28% PPL improvement over uniform 4-bit at 4× compression across Qwen2.5-7B, Llama-3.1-8B, Mistral-7B-v0.3\n3. Cardinality constraint is essential—unconstrained MCKP catastrophically degrades (3.3× worse than uniform on Qwen)\n\n## Method Details\n\n- Per-head distortion profiling: For each head i and bit-width b, measure δ_{i,b} = PPL(b^16_{-i}, b_i=b) - PPL(b^16). Cost: 4N forward passes.\n- MCKP formulation: min Σ d_{i,b_i} s.t. Σ c_{i,b_i} ≤ B (budget), Σ 1[b_i=2] ≤ k (cardinality), b_i ∈ {2,4,8,16}\n- Cardinality bound k determined empirically from compounding curve elbow (sweep k from 0 to N)\n- Solver: LP relaxation with rounding, <0.05s wall-clock\n\n## Experiments Presented (10 experiments in the paper)\n\n1. **Main results** (Table 1): 3 models × 4× compression. Constrained MCKP: Qwen 11.08 (28.3% impr), Llama 6.78 (24.2%), Mistral 7.68 (22.2%). Unconstrained degrades catastrophically.\n2. **Allocation detail** (Table 2): Llama breakdown—16@2bit, 192@4bit, 32@8bit, 16@16bit. 3.97× compression.\n3. **Compounding sweep** (Table 3, Figure 1): k from 0→112 on Qwen. Elbow at k≈12, optimal k=8. Pre-elbow slope 0.055, post-elbow 1.29, ratio 23×, SNR=7.3.\n4. **Budget sweep** (Table 4): 2×/4×/8× compression. Improvement grows: 1.8%→28.3%→63.4%.\n5. **RateQuant comparison** (Table 5): On Llama, RateQuant rounded PPL=8.12 (9.3% impr) vs MCKP 6.78 (24.2%). RateQuant assigns 38@2bit, only 5/8 L0 protected.\n6. **Oracle comparison** (Table 6): 4 layer groups brute-force. Gap <1% (0.2-0.6%).\n7. **Cross-dataset robustness** (Table 7): WikiText-2 vs C4 cross-calibration. PPL gap only 0.13. Rank correlation ρ=0.94, 90.2% allocation agreement.\n8. **L0 auto-discovery** (Table 8): Remove L0 exclusion → solver auto-assigns 8-bit to all 4 L0 heads. 96.4% allocation agreement, PPL diff 0.05.\n9. **High-res profiling** (Table 9): 16× more data → mid-layer delta std +1400%, 14 new sensitive heads, PPL 10.72 (-3.2%). L0 delta changes only 2.1%.\n10. **Downstream tasks** (Table 10): MMLU 69.7 (vs FP16 71.2), LongBench 45.8 (vs FP16 48.3). Recovery 72-78%.\n11. **72B scaling** (Table 11): Profiling-only. L0 dominance 142× (vs 100× at 7B), top sensitive 2.8%, contribution 74%.\n\n## Experiment Design Reference (imagined experiments from plan)\n\nThe full experiment plan included 13 experiments. The paper covers all 13 (some as appendix stubs—Appendix A \"Layer 0 Sensitivity Across Models\" and Appendix B \"Discovered Mid-Layer Sensitive Heads\" are listed as headers only with no content).\n\n## Your Task\n\nProvide a detailed review covering:\n\n1. **Methodology Assessment**: Is the MCKP formulation sound? Is the profiling approach reasonable? Are there methodological gaps?\n2. **Experimental Rigor**: Are experiments sufficient and well-designed? Statistical significance? Missing controls? Reproducibility concerns?\n3. **Specific Issues**: Identify concrete problems with numbers, methodology, or experimental design.\n4. **Strengths and Weaknesses**: Bulleted lists.\n5. **Questions for Authors**: Specific questions you'd want answered.\n6. **Preliminary Score**: On the 6-point scale with justification.\n\nBe rigorous and specific. Reference table/section numbers. Consider whether the evidence actually supports the claims.","agentId":"a9d9be3d2efbc27d6","agentType":"general-purpose","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Review submitted. Here is the summary:\n\n**Score: 4 (Weak Accept)**\n\nKey findings from the Methods and Experiments review:\n\n**Critical data integrity issues:**\n- Cross-referencing the paper's Table 3 against the actual `compounding_sweep_results.csv` reveals systematic discrepancies. Most notably, k=0 is reported as PPL=12.01 but actual data shows 15.4454 (equal to uniform 4-bit), and k=75/k=112 show identical actual values (35.348) but different paper values.\n- Only ~3 of 11 tables are grounded in real experimental data. All Llama, Mistral, C4, downstream, oracle, and RateQuant results are imagined via the dream framework.\n- The headline Qwen PPL uses 11.08 (imagined) instead of the actual 11.23.\n\n**Missing critical baseline:**\n- Project records show \"protecting Layer 0 alone reduces PPL from 15.45 to 10.62\" -- this trivial heuristic outperforms the constrained MCKP (11.23). This comparison is absent from the paper and potentially undermines Claim 2.\n\n**Methodological gaps:**\n- No quantization scheme specified (RTN/GPTQ/symmetric/asymmetric)\n- No statistical significance (single-seed, no error bars)\n- Cardinality bound k is positioned as principled but requires a hyperparameter sweep\n- Arithmetic errors in imagined data (Llama compression ratio computes to 3.12x, not 3.97x)\n- Elbow slope values (0.055 and 1.29) don't match the paper's own table values\n\n**Strengths that support conditional acceptance:**\n- The compounding effect observation is genuinely novel and the actual sweep data does show a clear elbow\n- MCKP formulation is clean and computationally efficient\n- L0 auto-discovery experiment was actually run and is convincing\n- The idea addresses a real gap in hardware-aligned discrete bit-width allocation"}],"totalDurationMs":199213,"totalTokens":103406,"totalToolUseCount":23,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4939,"cache_read_input_tokens":97985,"output_tokens":481,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":4939},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":1,"output_tokens":481,"cache_read_input_tokens":97985,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4939,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":4939,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"toolStats":{"readCount":7,"searchCount":1,"bashCount":0,"editFileCount":0,"linesAdded":0,"linesRemoved":0,"otherToolCount":15}}}
{"type":"system","subtype":"task_progress","task_id":"a4e14256134443d34","tool_use_id":"toolu_01TPXVEqBEghLnqCMbFFptVS","description":"R2: Novelty + Related Work review","usage":{"total_tokens":96076,"tool_uses":21,"duration_ms":182037},"last_tool_name":"mcp__agent-ml-research__submit_dream_review_report","uuid":"c626a939-f6fd-43ed-b9a7-aee1639aff6d","session_id":"69b15b48-5903-4373-bd27-49d1e8a602b8"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_019QHM9H671djRQ1z5BpAvFF","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01MH1swx8V93EtwTx8hzREUW","name":"mcp__agent-ml-research__submit_dream_review_report","input":{"project":"ratekv","tier":"best","dream_id":"dream_20260514T002109","overall_score":"Weak Accept (4)","report":"# Reviewer R2: Novelty, Positioning, and Related Work\n\n## Paper: \"Cardinality-Constrained MCKP for Per-Head Mixed-Precision KV Cache Quantization\"\n\n---\n\n## 1. Novelty Assessment\n\n### Claim 1: Head sensitivity heterogeneity (Layer 0 dominance)\n\n**Novelty: Low.** The observation that different layers/heads have different quantization sensitivity is well-established in the mixed-precision quantization literature. HAWQ (NeurIPS 2019/2020) demonstrated Hessian-based sensitivity heterogeneity for weight quantization. KIVI (ICML 2024) already noted non-uniform sensitivity across KV cache dimensions. The specific observation that Layer 0 is extremely sensitive (~100x) is empirically interesting but not conceptually surprising — early layers process raw embeddings and are known to be critical across many perturbation studies (e.g., layer pruning literature consistently finds early and late layers most important). This is a useful empirical characterization, not a novel insight.\n\n### Claim 2: MCKP formulation for discrete bit-width allocation\n\n**Novelty: Low-to-Moderate.** MCKP is a textbook combinatorial optimization formulation. Applying it to bit-width allocation is a natural modeling choice — HAQ (CVPR 2019) already framed mixed-precision weight quantization as a resource allocation problem (solved via RL), and HAWQ used Hessian-based criteria for the same purpose. The move from continuous R-D (RateQuant) to discrete MCKP is technically correct but algorithmically straightforward: the problem has only 4 options per item with a linear budget constraint, solved by LP relaxation in <0.05s. There is no algorithmic novelty in the solver itself.\n\n### Claim 3: Cardinality constraint as compounding regularization\n\n**Novelty: Moderate-to-High.** This is the paper's genuine contribution. The specific insight chain is: (a) independent per-head profiling assumes additive distortion, (b) this assumption breaks catastrophically when many heads are simultaneously assigned extreme quantization (demonstrated by PPL 36.92 for unconstrained vs 11.08 constrained), (c) cardinality constraints on 2-bit assignments regularize this non-additivity, and (d) the compounding threshold can be empirically determined via an elbow curve with a sharp transition (SNR=7.3, slope ratio 23x). The framing of cardinality constraints as \"profiler error regularization\" rather than ad-hoc heuristic is a useful conceptual contribution. The compounding elbow curve with clear additive/superlinear regimes is the paper's strongest novel finding.\n\n**Overall novelty verdict:** The paper's novelty rests almost entirely on Claim 3. Claims 1 and 2 are incremental applications of known ideas to a new domain. The question is whether the compounding observation + cardinality regularization is sufficient novelty for a top venue. I lean toward \"yes, barely\" — it is a genuine insight that changes how one should think about mixed-precision allocation, not just a better algorithm for the same formulation.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Related Work Completeness\n\n### Missing or insufficiently discussed prior art:\n\n**(a) GEAR (ICML 2024):** Applies low-rank + quantization residual for KV cache compression, achieving near-lossless compression at very low bits. This is a fundamentally different approach (approximation-based rather than allocation-based) but highly relevant as a baseline and for positioning.\n\n**(b) CLA / Cross-Layer Attention (2024):** Shares KV cache across layers, reducing total cache size. Orthogonal to per-head quantization but should be discussed as complementary.\n\n**(c) Atom / QServe / QuaRot (2024-2025):** More recent quantization kernels that support mixed-precision at the hardware level. The paper's {2,4,8,16} action set implicitly assumes hardware support — this deserves discussion.\n\n**(d) SqueezeLLM (ICML 2024):** Non-uniform quantization with sensitivity-based allocation for weights. The sensitivity profiling methodology is similar.\n\n**(e) MiKV / MixedKV (if published by submission time):** Any concurrent work on per-head or per-layer mixed-precision KV cache quantization.\n\n**(f) DuoAttention (2024):** Cited in the project history (D001, D003) as providing streaming/retrieval head classification for Llama. Conspicuously absent from the paper despite being directly relevant — DuoAttention's distinction between \"streaming\" and \"retrieval\" heads is essentially a binary version of the sensitivity heterogeneity this paper measures continuously.\n\n**(g) Broader knapsack/resource-allocation literature in ML:** The paper should cite prior uses of MCKP or constrained knapsack formulations in neural architecture search, pruning, or mixed-precision — the formulation is not new to ML.\n\n### Assessment: The related work section is thin (only 6 citations in the bibliography). For a top venue, this needs significant expansion. The three-paragraph structure is adequate but the coverage is incomplete, particularly on recent KV cache optimization methods and the mixed-precision weight quantization literature that the MCKP formulation most directly builds on.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Differentiation from RateQuant\n\n### Fairness of comparison:\n\nThe comparison has **two significant fairness concerns**:\n\n**(a) RateQuant has a discrete greedy algorithm (Algorithm 1, Proposition 6)** that is optimal on consecutive integer bit-widths. The paper initially mischaracterized RateQuant as purely continuous (corrected in D012), but the current comparison still only evaluates \"RateQuant (rounded)\" — i.e., continuous waterfilling + rounding. This is the weakest possible instantiation of RateQuant. A fair comparison would also run RateQuant's discrete greedy on {2,4,8,16} and show whether its polymatroid-based optimality breaks on non-consecutive integers, as claimed. The paper asserts this theoretically but does not demonstrate it experimentally. This is a critical gap.\n\n**(b) RateQuant supports asymmetric K/V allocation** (e.g., K=2.85-bit, V=2.15-bit average). The paper claims MCKP \"naturally\" handles this by treating K and V as independent items, but no experiment validates this. If RateQuant's K/V separation provides meaningful gains, the comparison may be less favorable.\n\n**(c) The 16.5% gap (PPL 8.12 vs 6.78 on Llama) is partly attributable to compounding blindness** (38 heads at 2-bit vs 16), which could be addressed by adding a cardinality constraint to RateQuant's greedy algorithm — a trivial modification. The paper does not discuss whether the improvement is inherent to MCKP formulation vs. simply the cardinality constraint. An ablation of \"RateQuant greedy + cardinality constraint\" would clarify whether MCKP itself matters or whether the constraint is the entire contribution.\n\n### Adequacy of explanation:\n\nThe paper explains two factors (rounding loss, compounding blindness) but conflates them. A cleaner analysis would decompose the gap: how much comes from discrete-vs-continuous, and how much from constrained-vs-unconstrained? Without this decomposition, the reader cannot tell whether the MCKP formulation or the cardinality constraint is doing the work.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Broader Context\n\n### Missing connections:\n\n**(a) KV cache eviction/sparsity:** The paper mentions eviction was abandoned (Section 5, Limitations) but does not engage with the substantial eviction literature: H2O (Zhang et al., 2023), StreamingLLM (Xiao et al., 2023), SnapKV (Li et al., 2024), PyramidKV (2024). These are complementary: eviction reduces the number of KV entries, quantization reduces the bits per entry. A joint framework would be natural and the paper should discuss why it chose not to pursue this (beyond \"MSE doesn't work for eviction\" — there are PPL-based eviction methods).\n\n**(b) Quantization-aware training vs post-training:** The paper is entirely post-training quantization. How does the sensitivity profile change if the model is fine-tuned with quantization-aware training? This limitation should be acknowledged.\n\n**(c) Inference systems integration:** No discussion of how mixed-precision KV cache interacts with actual inference kernels (vLLM, TensorRT-LLM, SGLang). The {2,4,8,16} action set implies hardware support, but having 4 different precisions in the same batch may introduce kernel dispatch overhead. Practical deployment considerations are absent.\n\n**(d) Token-level vs head-level granularity:** Recent work (KVQuant, QJL) explores per-token or per-channel quantization. The paper's per-head granularity is coarser — is it complementary or competitive? Could you do per-head bit-width selection AND per-token quantization within each head?\n\n---\n\n## 5. Presentation and Clarity\n\n### Strengths:\n- The three-observation structure in the Introduction is effective and well-motivated.\n- The compounding effect sweep (Table 4 / Figure 1 placeholder) is the most compelling evidence and is well-presented.\n- The L0 auto-discovery experiment (Section 4.6) is a nice touch — showing the constraint is redundant strengthens the \"principled\" claim.\n- Honest reporting of limitations (8x compression still poor; profiling cost at 72B).\n\n### Weaknesses:\n- **The paper reads as a \"dream\" draft with imagined results.** Multiple experiments (Llama, Mistral, RateQuant comparison, oracle, downstream tasks, 72B scaling) appear to use projected/imagined data rather than actual experimental results. The compounding sweep CSV shows real data only for Qwen2.5-7B with max_2bit in {0,4,8,16,24,48,75,112}. The actual PPL values in the CSV (e.g., max_2bit=75: PPL=35.35, max_2bit=112: PPL=35.35) differ from the paper's reported values (30.12 and 36.92), raising data integrity concerns.\n- **Figure 1 is a text placeholder**, not an actual figure. For a submission, this is unacceptable.\n- **The bibliography has only 6 entries** with two using placeholder author names (\"ARKV Authors\", \"RateQuant Authors\"). This is clearly pre-submission state.\n- **Claim 1 overstates:** \"100x higher profiling delta\" — the actual data shows L0 delta ~2.1 vs mid-layer avg ~0.02, which is 100x, but the paper also says \"~26x\" in paper_framing.md (3.29 vs 0.128). These inconsistencies need reconciliation.\n- **The oracle comparison methodology is unclear.** 4^28 configurations per group is computationally infeasible to enumerate. The paper mentions \"sampled 10^6 + DP\" but does not explain how DP applies to a non-additive distortion problem (since the whole point is that distortion is non-additive).\n\n---\n\n## 6. Strengths and Weaknesses\n\n### Strengths:\n- The compounding effect observation and its characterization via the elbow curve is a genuine empirical contribution that other methods (including RateQuant) do not address.\n- The cardinality constraint framing as \"profiler error regularization\" is a useful conceptual reframing — it elevates what could be seen as a heuristic threshold into a principled design choice.\n- Cross-model consistency (if validated on actual runs) across three architectures with different GQA ratios is solid generalization evidence.\n- The solver is trivially fast (<0.05s), making it practical.\n- The L0 auto-discovery result elegantly demonstrates that the solver does not need manual architectural knowledge.\n- Cross-dataset robustness (rank correlation 0.94) supports the claim that sensitivity is an architectural property.\n- Honest about what does not work (eviction, 8x compression limits).\n\n### Weaknesses:\n- **The MCKP formulation itself is not novel** — it is a standard textbook optimization applied to a new domain. The algorithmic contribution is essentially zero.\n- **The cardinality constraint is a single hyperparameter k** determined by a separate sweep. While the elbow detection is clean on Qwen2.5-7B, the paper does not establish how robust this elbow detection is across models. Does the elbow always exist? Is the SNR always high? What happens if the elbow is ambiguous?\n- **The comparison with RateQuant is potentially unfair** — only the weakest variant (continuous + rounding) is evaluated. RateQuant's discrete greedy with a post-hoc cardinality constraint would be the real test.\n- **Critical experiments appear to be imagined/projected** rather than actually run (Llama, Mistral, RateQuant, downstream tasks based on dream report framework).\n- **Very narrow evaluation scope for the claimed contribution**: only perplexity on WikiText-2 as primary metric, only 7B-class models fully evaluated, no latency/throughput measurements.\n- **The paper does not discuss the interaction between quantization method (RTN? GPTQ? group size?) and the sensitivity profile.** Different quantization algorithms may produce different head-level sensitivity rankings. Are the profiles stable across quantization methods?\n- **Missing discussion of computational cost of profiling**: 4N forward passes (4 x 112 = 448 for Qwen, 4 x 256 = 1024 for Llama) on a calibration set. At what model scale does this become impractical? The 72B profiling takes 3.5h — what about 405B?\n\n---\n\n## 7. Questions for Authors\n\n1. **RateQuant greedy ablation:** Have you run RateQuant's discrete greedy algorithm (Algorithm 1) directly on {2,4,8,16}? If not, how can you claim MCKP is necessary rather than simply adding a cardinality constraint to any existing allocation method?\n\n2. **Cardinality constraint transferability:** You determine k=8 for Qwen2.5-7B via a sweep. For Llama and Mistral, how is k determined? Is there a model-agnostic rule (e.g., k = 7% of heads)?\n\n3. **Compounding elbow robustness:** The elbow is clean for Qwen, but does it always exist? Have you seen cases where the PPL-vs-k curve is smooth (no sharp transition)? What would the method do in that case?\n\n4. **Quantization algorithm dependence:** Your profiling uses a specific quantization kernel (presumably round-to-nearest per-group). Do sensitivity rankings change if you use GPTQ, AWQ, or different group sizes? If so, the profiles are not architecture-intrinsic but algorithm-dependent.\n\n5. **Data integrity question:** The compounding_sweep_results.csv shows max_2bit=75 and max_2bit=112 both yielding PPL=35.348 with identical allocations (75 heads at 2-bit, 25 at 4-bit, 0 at 8-bit, 12 at 16-bit). However, the paper reports PPL=30.12 for k=75 and PPL=36.92 for k=112. Which numbers are correct?\n\n6. **Oracle comparison methodology:** You claim <1% gap to brute-force oracle, but 4^28 = 7.2e16 configurations are infeasible to enumerate. You mention \"sampled 10^6 + DP.\" How does DP work here if distortion is non-additive (the paper's core claim)? Doesn't using DP assume additive distortion, contradicting the premise?\n\n7. **Why not per-token or per-channel mixed precision?** Head-level granularity is relatively coarse (112 items for Qwen). Have you considered finer-grained allocation within the MCKP framework?\n\n8. **Practical deployment:** How does a KV cache with 4 different bit-widths work in practice? Does this require custom CUDA kernels? What is the actual inference speedup/slowdown compared to uniform quantization?\n\n---\n\n## 8. Preliminary Score: 4 (Weak Accept)\n\n### Justification:\n\n**For acceptance (arguments for 4-5):**\n- The compounding effect observation and its characterization via the elbow curve is a real, reproducible finding that has practical implications for anyone doing mixed-precision KV cache quantization.\n- The cardinality constraint as principled regularization is a modest but genuine conceptual contribution — it is not just \"applying MCKP\" but identifying why naive application fails and how to fix it.\n- If the cross-model results hold up, the consistency across three architectures is solid.\n- The paper is well-structured and the story arc (heterogeneity exists -> naive exploitation fails -> regularized exploitation succeeds) is compelling.\n\n**Against acceptance (arguments for 3):**\n- The MCKP formulation is textbook — the algorithmic contribution is nil.\n- The comparison with RateQuant may be unfair (weakest variant only).\n- Much of the experimental evidence appears to be projected rather than actually collected, raising serious concerns about the paper's readiness.\n- Related work is thin (6 citations) and misses several important baselines.\n- The contribution may reduce to \"add a cardinality constraint to any mixed-precision allocation method\" — a useful trick but potentially below the novelty bar for a top venue.\n\n**Net assessment:** The compounding effect finding and its operationalization via cardinality constraints is a genuine, if modest, contribution. The paper needs stronger baselines (especially RateQuant greedy + cardinality), complete real experiments, expanded related work, and a clearer decomposition of where gains come from (MCKP vs constraint). In its current state it is borderline. If the cross-model results and RateQuant comparison hold up with actual data and the related work is expanded, this is a solid workshop-to-main-track paper. The 11-day timeline to ARR is very tight for the missing experiments.\n\n**Confidence: 4/5** (familiar with KV cache quantization literature and MCKP formulations; have not read the RateQuant paper in detail to fully assess the greedy algorithm comparison)."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":734,"cache_read_input_tokens":94835,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":734,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01TPXVEqBEghLnqCMbFFptVS","session_id":"69b15b48-5903-4373-bd27-49d1e8a602b8","uuid":"7c741ae5-13a1-43e0-b819-f43817a71d93"}
